<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- What differs per package; the rest is in src/Directory.Build.props -->
    <PackageId>Dahlke.EtherCAT.Diagnostics</PackageId>
    <Description>EtherCAT master and slave diagnostics over TwinCAT ADS — topology scanning, slave and port state, CRC and frame error counters, sync-unit faults, and a change-event stream, read through raw ADS index groups rather than the symbol API.</Description>
    <PackageTags>TwinCAT;ADS;EtherCAT;Beckhoff;PLC;Automation;Diagnostics;Fieldbus</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The adsify original also granted this to Adsify.Tests, whose fixtures reach in for
      EtherCatClient.BuildPortInfo and EtherCatCache. That grant is dropped here: nothing in this
      repository can build Adsify.Tests, so it would be inert and unverifiable. When adsify switches
      to consuming this as a package, those two sites need either decoupling or the grant restored.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Dahlke.EtherCAT.Diagnostics.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Dahlke.EtherCAT.Esi/Dahlke.EtherCAT.Esi.csproj" />
    <!--
      A ProjectReference now, where the adsify original carried a PackageReference to
      Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads 0.8.0. That removed SOME of the version pinning the original needed, but
      not all of it — see the note below the Beckhoff reference for the half that survives, and why.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads/Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="4.14.0">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <!--
      Bracketed to match the ceiling Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads sets on the same package, so this one cannot
      resolve a Beckhoff version the library it builds on forbids. Why that ceiling exists, and why
      the Microsoft.Extensions references are deliberately NOT bracketed, are in
      Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads.csproj and the ROOT Directory.Build.props respectively.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Beckhoff.TwinCAT.Ads" Version="[7.0.292,8.0.0)" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="$(MicrosoftExtensionsVersion)" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="$(MicrosoftExtensionsVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    These two references cannot use $(MicrosoftExtensionsVersion), and their versions are
    deliberately not round numbers. Both are declared rather than left transitive because both are
    contracts this package's PUBLIC surface uses directly: AddEtherCatDiagnostics takes and returns
    IServiceCollection, and the client and monitor take an ILogger.

    Beckhoff.TwinCAT.Ads 7.0.292 imposes a transitive FLOOR on Logging.Abstractions — >= 8.0.3 on
    net8.0 and >= 10.0.9 on net10.0, read from that package's own nuspec dependency groups — and a
    direct pin below a floor is an NU1605 downgrade ERROR, not a warning. $(MicrosoftExtensionsVersion)
    is 8.0.0/9.0.0/10.0.0, so two of the three legs fail the build.

    Then the floor CASCADES: Logging.Abstractions 8.0.3 itself requires
    DependencyInjection.Abstractions >= 8.0.2, and 10.0.9 requires >= 10.0.9, so declaring that one at
    the shared version fails the same two legs for a second reason. Both cascade steps were measured
    while adopting this package — each was a build failure first and a pin second.

    net9.0 is 9.0.0 for both, and that is NOT a floor being cleared: Beckhoff 7.0.292 ships no net9.0
    dependency group, so net9.0 resolves against the net8.0 one and the floor there is 8.0.3, which
    9.0.0 clears trivially. The conditional exists so a net9 consumer sees a 9.x floor rather than an
    8.x one, keeping the per-target-framework policy the root Directory.Build.props states.

    Note what this is NOT: none of it is a consequence of this repository. The floors come from
    BECKHOFF, so they survived the move from a PackageReference to a ProjectReference on
    Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads. The floors that DID go away with that conversion were the Dahlke-derived ones
    the adsify original also had to clear (Hosting.Abstractions >= 8.0.1, Options >= 8.0.2), which is
    why those two references sit on the shared property above.

    Dahlke.TwinCAT.Ads itself never trips any of this despite referencing the same Beckhoff package,
    because it uses ILogger and IServiceCollection without declaring either abstraction directly — it
    has no pin to conflict, and NuGet simply resolves Beckhoff's floor. Declaring them is the more
    honest thing for a published package, and this comment is what it costs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net8.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="8.0.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="8.0.3" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net9.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="9.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="9.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.9" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
